          "message": "feat(resource): input validation + OpenAPI 3.1 for auto-CRUD\n\n* feat(openapi): resource operation metadata + generic component-name sanitizer\n\nAdd resourceOp to routeInfo so kruda.Resource auto-CRUD routes can render\nexplicit OpenAPI operations (list/get/create/update/delete) with correct\n200/201/204 status codes, page/limit query params, request body, a 422 only\nwhen validation is engaged, and the same default error response as typed\nroutes. Typed routes (resourceOp==nil) render through the unchanged path.\n\nSanitize generic instantiation component names (e.g.\nResourceList[github.com/app/models.User] -> ResourceList_User) so the emitted\n$ref is a valid JSON pointer. Non-generic names are unchanged and the existing\npkgPath-based collision disambiguation is preserved.\n\n* feat(resource): validation, pagination 400s, ResourceList envelope, OpenAPI\n\nWire kruda.Resource auto-CRUD into the typed-handler contract:\n\n- create/update validate the decoded body via buildValidators/validate,\n  gated identically to typed routes (Validator configured AND T has tags) →\n  422 on failure; malformed JSON now → 400 \"invalid request body\" (was 500)\n  via wrapBindErr, which preserves existing *KrudaError (incl. 413).\n- list pagination rejects invalid page/limit with 400 (typed message format),\n  defaults to page=1/limit=20 when absent, clamps limit to maxResourceListLimit\n  (100); returns the new public ResourceList[T] envelope (identical JSON).\n- every registered op contributes a routeInfo (with resourceOp) appended inside\n  the same resourceShouldRegister branch as its route, so OpenAPI mirrors the\n  router under only/except; consumed by openapi.go's buildResourceOperation.\n- registerResource takes (app, pathPrefix) plain params (no routeRegistrar\n  growth); OpenAPI paths use joinPath so Group prefixes match the radix routes.\n- unsupported ID kinds panic at registration (string/int/int64/uint/uint64).\n\nService errors still propagate unchanged; no framework-synthesized 404.\n\n* fix(openapi): full-qualified component keys so distinct generic args never collide\n\nThe component-name sanitizer collapsed each generic type argument to its short\nidentifier, so ResourceList[pkgA.User] and ResourceList[pkgB.User] both keyed to\nResourceList_User; the second schema was dropped and its $ref pointed at the\nfirst. The collision guard compared t.PkgPath(), but for a generic instantiation\nthat is always the envelope's package, never the type arg's, so it never fired.\nNested-bracket args (map[string]int) also left [ ] in the key.\n\nSanitize the full instantiation name instead: every run of /.[]* and spaces\nbecomes one _, with a _Ptr token per pointer star so value and pointer args stay\ndistinct. The transform only merges already-illegal punctuation, so it is\ninjective for distinct package paths, arg names, and value-vs-pointer. Non-generic\nnames are returned unchanged (golden guard relies on the no-op). Cross-package\nsame-short-name plain types are disambiguated by a typeID-based collision check.\n\n* fix(resource): make named string/integer ID types work end-to-end\n\nThe registration gate switches on idType.Kind(), so a named type like\ntype UserID int64 (Kind Int64) passed registration, but resourceParseID switched\non the concrete type and had no case for it, so every get/update/delete returned\na runtime 400 invalid id. Widen resourceParseID to switch on reflect.Kind\n(matching the gate), parse via strconv, and convert back with reflect Convert so\nnamed string/integer/unsigned IDs round-trip. The Kind-based gate is unchanged.\n\nAlso add the missing partial-PUT test: a PUT body that omits a required field\nmust 422 (the surprising behavior change flagged in the spec).\n\n* fix(openapi): collision-proof component keys for 3+ same-segment packages\n\nThe generic component-name sanitizer disambiguated a cross-package short-name\nclash by appending only the last package segment, in a single attempt that\nfell through to overwrite on a second clash. Three+ plain named types sharing\nboth a short name and a trailing segment (e.g. several \".../models.User\")\ntherefore collapsed: the second schema was silently dropped and its $ref\nmis-pointed at the third.\n\nDisambiguate with the full sanitized package path, then a numeric counter,\nnever overwriting an existing key whose type identity differs — so distinct\ntypes always get distinct keys.\n\nAlso generalize sanitizeComponentName to collapse any non-key-safe rune\n(notably the \",\" reflect emits between multiple type args, and parentheses in\nfunc-typed args) instead of an enumerated set, while preserving \"-\" (legal in\ncomponent keys and present in module paths like \"go-kruda\"). This keeps the\nkey→type mapping injective and the produced key valid per the OpenAPI\ncomponent-name pattern; plain identifiers are still returned unchanged, so the\ntyped-only golden snapshot is unaffected.\n\nAdd regression tests: a 3-way same-segment collision, a multi-arg generic\n(comma) key, and hyphen preservation.\n\n* fix(resource): width-aware ID parse + hoist parser, unify gate/parser kinds\n\nresourceParseID parsed every integer ID at a fixed 64-bit width and then\nnarrowed to the ID type with reflect.Convert, which truncates silently. On\n32-bit builds (e.g. js/wasm) an out-of-range `int`/`uint` id would wrap to a\nwrong value and return 200 instead of the 400 an out-of-range id should yield.\nDerive the parse bit width from the ID type (idType.Bits()) so strconv rejects\noverflow up front and the conversion is always lossless.\n\nThe registration gate and the parser switched on reflect.Kind in two places\nwith different sets: the parser handled int8/16/32 and uint8/16/32 arms the\ngate never admits, so those arms were unreachable dead code and a latent trap\n(widening the gate would have made the truncation reachable on all platforms).\nIntroduce a single resourceIDKindSupported predicate consulted by the gate and\ntrim the parser to the same {string,int,int64,uint,uint64} set, so the two\ncannot drift. int32 (and other narrow widths) still panic at registration as\nbefore.\n\nHoist the reflect.Type lookup out of the per-request path: buildResourceIDParser\ncomputes the parser once at registration and the GET/PUT/DELETE handlers call\nit directly, removing a per-request reflect.TypeOf. resourceParseID remains a\nthin wrapper for direct callers. The resource path is opt-in and off the\nframework hot path; this only removes avoidable per-request work.\n\nAdd regression tests: out-of-range id -> 400, and gate/parser kind agreement.",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-21T21:48:33+07:00",
